 "user_scenario": "You are Marcus Albright, Denise Albright's son. Your mother is 65 and has been at the Keystone Ridge center in Lancaster, room 127-A, since late October, recovering after a hospital stay; you handle her bills, the business office has you on file as her representative, and the phone number on her account, 717-555-0168, is your cell. You are calling on a weekday morning from your desk at work, calm and methodical, and you jot things down as you go. You open by saying: \"I'd like to pay my mother's December Medicare coinsurance by bank draft from my checking account today.\" You know her new Medigap plan only started partway through December, so you expect she owes something for the first part of the month; you have not paid anything toward December yet, you have no figure in mind, and you want it settled now so it is off your list. Paying what your mother owes for December is the only thing you want from this call.\n\nWhen the agent asks who the resident is, you give her full name, Denise Albright. When the agent needs to confirm details, you give her date of birth, 1960-06-10 (June 10, 1960), and your phone number, 717-555-0168; only if the agent asks for something more do you add the last four digits of her Social Security number, 1738, or your email, marcus.albright@comcast.net. If the agent asks about her supplemental insurance, you say her Medigap policy is with Conestoga Mutual Medigap, that she moved from Plan K to Plan N during her Medigap open enrollment, and that the new plan started on December 10. If the agent asks whether she was away from the center in December, you say she spent the afternoon and night of December 1 at your house in Lititz and came back the next day, and you told the center not to hold her bed; she was taken to Lancaster General Hospital on the evening of December 3 and came back on December 5, and that time you asked the center to hold her room; and on the evening of December 6 she was admitted to Lancaster General Hospital again overnight and came back on the 7th, and you did not hold the bed that night. If the agent asks whether it may take care of whatever the business office needs to do on her account so that December can be paid, you say yes, go ahead.\n\nWhen the agent tells you the amount, you accept it; you have nothing to compare it with and never suggest a figure of your own. If the agent asks for your bank routing or account number, you say you would rather enter them yourself through the bank-draft payment the agent hands you. When the agent hands the bank-draft payment over to you, you complete it yourself for exactly the amount and the charge the agent gives you. Once the payment has gone through and the agent confirms it, you thank them and say goodbye.",
